This guide explains how to set up your computer so that you can

run the notebooks of the book and change them;

build the whole book as a website, with every output and figure already computed;

publish that website on GitHub Pages, without any computation on GitHub.

You only need steps 1 and 2 to study the book. Step 3 is for those who want to publish their own copy (for example, the author, or a student who forks the repository).

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

explain what it means for a program to learn from data, and tell the main kinds of learning problems apart (regression, classification, clustering, reinforcement learning);

represent a dataset as a matrix \mathbf{X} and a vector \mathbf{y}, and read their shapes;

formulate learning as the minimization of a loss, and compute a baseline to compare against;

derive the normal equations of least squares, interpret them geometrically, and verify them in code;

explain overfitting and underfitting, and why a model must be evaluated on data it has not seen;

run a complete workflow split → preprocess → fit → evaluate with scikit-learn;

set up the environment, run the notebooks, and build and publish this book as a static website (see the 

Setup Guide).

Prerequisites. Vectors, matrices, dot products, the gradient. Nothing else.

How to read this chapter. The first sections build intuition; Sections 1.6–1.8 are the technical core; the exercises at the end are the best way to check your understanding. Every code cell can be executed, and changed, in your own copy of the notebook.

Chapter 1 showed the whole story of machine learning in miniature. Before we study methods one by one, we make sure that the tools underneath them are solid. A large part of the bugs in machine learning code are not “machine learning” bugs at all: they are a wrong array shape, a silent broadcast, a feature on the wrong scale, or a test sample that leaked into the preprocessing. This chapter is about avoiding those.

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

create, index, and reshape NumPy arrays, and explain the difference between a view and a copy;

write vectorized code and use broadcasting correctly, checking shapes before trusting results;

use numpy.linalg for the operations that machine learning needs (solving systems, norms, eigendecompositions), and recognize the main numerical traps;

load, inspect, filter, group, and summarize tabular data with pandas;

produce clear, honest figures with the object-oriented interface of Matplotlib;

use the language of probability: conditional probability and Bayes’ rule, random variables, expectation, variance, covariance, the Gaussian distribution (univariate and multivariate), the law of large numbers and the central limit theorem;

derive the maximum likelihood estimates of a Bernoulli and a Gaussian model, and explain why least squares is maximum likelihood under Gaussian noise;

preprocess data: scale features, transform skewed ones, impute missing values, encode categories, and assemble everything in a leak-free scikit-learn pipeline.

Prerequisites. Chapter 1. Basic programming (variables, loops, functions); the next section is a five-minute refresher in case it has been a while. Calculus of one variable and the matrix notation of Chapter 1.

How to read this chapter. It is a toolbox, not a story, so you can read it in pieces: Sections 2.3 to 2.5 are about software, Section 2.6 about probability, and Section 2.7 ties everything together with a real preprocessing pipeline. If you already know NumPy and pandas, skim Sections 2.3 and 2.4, but do not skip the Pitfall boxes: they describe mistakes that experienced people still make.

In Chapter 1 we trained models and measured them on data they had not seen. In Chapter 2 we learned that an average over a sample is an estimate of an expectation. This chapter puts the two ideas together. It answers a question that every method in the rest of the book silently relies on: why should a function that has a small error on the training set have a small error on future data? The answer is not “always”: we will see exactly when it holds, how fast it improves with the number of examples, and what it depends on.

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

define the data distribution, the expected risk and the empirical risk, and explain why the whole of machine learning is about the gap between them;

derive the Bayes-optimal predictor for the squared loss and for the 0–1 loss, and explain why a part of the error can never be removed;

write empirical risk minimization (ERM) and split the excess risk into an approximation error and an estimation error;

explain, with a short proof and with experiments, why the training error is optimistic, and quantify the optimism when many candidate models are compared;

state Markov’s, Chebyshev’s and Hoeffding’s inequalities, and derive Hoeffding’s inequality;

prove a generalization bound for a finite hypothesis class with the union bound, and turn it into a sample-complexity formula;

define shattering, the growth function and the VC dimension, compute them for simple classes, and state the VC generalization bound;

define the Rademacher complexity, derive its bound for norm-bounded linear classifiers, and explain why controlling the norm (regularization, large margins) controls generalization even when the dimension is larger than the number of examples;

state the No Free Lunch theorem and explain what an inductive bias is;

use Hoeffding’s inequality to size a test set, and judge honestly how loose the bounds of the theory are on a real data set.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 and 2, in particular Sections 1.5 (loss and risk) and 2.6 (expectation, variance, the law of large numbers). Calculus and the matrix notation of the previous chapters. No measure theory: random variables are treated as in Chapter 2.

How to read this chapter. It is the most mathematical chapter of Part I, and it is built so that you can read it at two depths. Each section has the same rhythm: an idea in words, a precise statement, a proof (marked, and safe to skip at a first reading), and an experiment that makes the statement concrete. The experiments carry the message; the proofs explain why the message is true. If you skip every proof you will still be able to follow the book. If you read them all, you will have seen the three classical ways of measuring the capacity of a model: its size (Section 3.6), its shattering power (Section 3.7) and its ability to fit noise (Section 3.8).

A word about the data in this chapter

Theory talks about a distribution \mathcal{D} that is unknown in practice. To check a statement about \mathcal{D} numerically we need a world where we do know it. In Sections 3.2, 3.3 and 3.4 we therefore use a small simulated world, and from Section 3.6 on we also use a real data set (Breast Cancer, seen in Chapter 1), treated as the population from which we draw samples. Whenever a number comes from a simulation, the text says so.

Section 3.4 showed that Hoeffding’s guarantee for one fixed function cannot be applied to \hat f_S, because \hat f_S was chosen with the help of the data. The remedy is to ask for more: a bound that holds simultaneously for all functions of \mathcal{H}, hence also for the one that the data selected. For a finite class the union bound does it: for any events A_1, \dots, A_m,\mathbb{P}\Big(\bigcup_{j=1}^m A_j\Big) \le \sum_{j=1}^m \mathbb{P}(A_j).

Let \mathcal{H} be a finite set with \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert functions and let the loss take values in [0, 1].

Theorem 3.1 (uniform deviation for a finite class). For every \delta \in (0, 1), with probability at least 1 - \delta over the draw of the training set S of n i.i.d. examples, all functions f \in \mathcal{H} satisfy\boxed{\ \lvert R(f) - \hat{R}_S(f)\rvert \le \sqrt{\frac{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)}{2n}}\ }

Proof. Let \varepsilon denote the right-hand side. For each fixed f, Hoeffding’s inequality gives \mathbb{P}(\lvert R(f) - \hat{R}_S(f)\rvert > \varepsilon) \le 2e^{-2n\varepsilon^2} = \delta/\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert, because 2n\varepsilon^2 = \ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta). By the union bound over the \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert functions, the probability that at least one of them deviates by more than \varepsilon is at most \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert \cdot \delta / \lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert = \delta. \square

The price of uniformity is the term \ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert: the size of the class enters only through its logarithm. A class with a million functions costs \ln 10^6 \approx 13.8; a class with 

10100 functions costs 230. This is the mathematical form of the optimism of Section 3.4.1, and it explains why the curves of Figure 3.4 grew like \sqrt{\ln m}.

Corollary 3.2 (what ERM guarantees). On the event of Theorem 3.1, with \varepsilon its right-hand side, the ERM solution satisfiesR(\hat f_S) \le R(f_{\mathcal{H}}) + 2\varepsilon, \qquad\text{that is,}\qquad \text{estimation error} \le 2\sqrt{\frac{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)}{2n}}.

Proof. Three inequalities, in a chain: R(\hat f_S) \le \hat{R}_S(\hat f_S) + \varepsilon (uniform deviation, applied to \hat f_S), then \hat{R}_S(\hat f_S) \le \hat{R}_S(f_{\mathcal{H}}) (definition of ERM), then \hat{R}_S(f_{\mathcal{H}}) \le R(f_{\mathcal{H}}) + \varepsilon (uniform deviation, applied to f_{\mathcal{H}}). \square

So the estimation error of ERM over a finite class shrinks like \sqrt{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert / n}: the more candidates, the more data are needed, but only logarithmically in the number of candidates. Inverting the formulas gives the sample complexity: to have all deviations at most \varepsilon, or an estimation error at most \varepsilon (which needs deviations of \varepsilon/2), it suffices thatn \ge \frac{\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)}{2\varepsilon^2}
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
n \ge \frac{2\,\big(\ln\lvert\mathcal{H}\rvert + \ln(2/\delta)\big)}{\varepsilon^2}
\quad\text{respectively.}

Chapter 3 showed that learning is possible when the class of functions is not too rich, and that the price of richness is a gap between training and test error. This chapter makes that statement concrete with the simplest learning method there is. The k-nearest-neighbor (k-NN) method has no training phase and no parameters to fit: to predict at a new point it looks at the k closest training points and averages their labels. Despite its simplicity it is a serious method (it is still used, and it is hard to beat on some problems), it is completely analyzable, and its single hyperparameter k moves smoothly from a very flexible model (k = 1) to a very rigid one (k = n). That makes it the ideal laboratory for the bias–variance trade-off, the idea that organizes the whole of Part II.

After working through this chapter you will be able to:

write the k-NN classifier and k-NN regressor, implement them from scratch with the vectorized distances of Chapter 2, and check your code against scikit-learn;

explain how k controls the flexibility of the decision boundary and of the fitted curve, and why the training error can never be used to choose it;

derive the bias–variance decomposition of the squared error, and use it to compute exactly the bias and the variance of k-NN regression;

explain, and verify by experiment, why the best k grows with the sample size while the neighborhoods shrink;

state the Cover–Hart theorem (the 1-NN error is at most twice the Bayes error) and the consistency of k-NN when k \to \infty and k/n \to 0;

describe the curse of dimensionality in three ways (empty neighborhoods, concentration of distances, slow rates), measure it on real data, and say what helps;

choose a distance and a weighting scheme sensibly, and explain when tree-based neighbor search (KD-trees, ball trees) is faster than brute force and when it is not.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 3. In particular the vectorized distances of Worked example 2.1 and Exercise 2.7, the standardization of Section 2.7, and the Bayes predictor and the approximation–estimation split of Sections 3.2 and 3.3.

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets that ship with scikit-learn: Digits (images of handwritten digits), Wine, Diabetes and Breast Cancer. To check the bias–variance formulas exactly we need a world in which the true function is known, so Sections 4.5 and 4.6 and part of Section 4.7 use simulated data. Whenever that is the case, the text says so.

Chapter 3 proved that the training error is an optimistic estimate of the true risk, and that the optimism grows with the number of candidates among which we choose. Chapter 4 ended with a method whose single knob, k, must be set with data that the model has not seen. This chapter turns those two lessons into a practice: the set of habits, procedures and numbers that separate a machine-learning result you can trust from one that only looks good. Almost every embarrassing failure of machine learning in applications (a model that works in the lab and not in the clinic, a competition winner that collapses on the final data, a paper whose accuracy cannot be reproduced) is a failure of the material of this chapter, not of the algorithms.

After working through it you will be able to:

explain the roles of the training, validation and test sets, and why the test set may be used once;

quantify the noise of a hold-out estimate, and measure the winner’s curse that appears when we choose the best of many candidates on the same data;

write K-fold cross-validation from scratch, state what it really estimates, and describe how its bias and its variance depend on K;

choose the right kind of split (stratified, repeated, grouped, time-ordered) and keep every data-dependent step inside the pipeline, so that nothing leaks;

tune hyperparameters with grid search and random search, apply the one-standard-error rule, and estimate the performance of the whole tuning procedure with nested cross-validation;

decide whether one model is really better than another, with McNemar’s test and the corrected resampled t-test, and know why the naive tests are wrong;

compute and interpret the confusion matrix, precision, recall, F-scores, ROC and precision–recall curves, AUC and calibration, and choose a decision threshold from the costs of the two kinds of error;

evaluate multiclass and regression models, always against a baseline, and follow a complete protocol from raw data to a reported number.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 4. In particular the leakage and the pipelines of Section 2.7, the optimism of the best of m candidates (Section 3.4.1) and its bound (Theorem 3.1), the sizing of a test set (Section 3.10.2), and the k-NN classifier of Chapter 4, which serves as the model to be tuned in most of the experiments.

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets that ship with scikit-learn: Wine, Digits, Breast Cancer and Diabetes. Three experiments need a situation in which the truth is known exactly, and they are flagged where they occur: the examples with coin-flip labels (Sections 5.4.3 and 5.5.2), in which the features are real but the labels are fair random coin flips, so that no model can have a true accuracy different from \tfrac12; the simulated patients of Section 5.3.3; and the schematic of random search in Section 5.4.2.

Grid search reports best_score_, the cross-validated score of the winning configuration. This is the best of 60 (or 25, or 1,000) noisy numbers computed on the same data, so we are back to Section 5.2.3: best_score_ is optimistic. To estimate the performance of the whole procedure (“run the grid search with 5-fold CV and refit the winner”) honestly, the selection must be repeated inside an outer loop on data that did not take part in it. This is nested cross-validation:

split the data into K_{\text{out}} outer folds;

for each outer fold: run the complete hyperparameter search (with its own inner cross-validation) on the other K_{\text{out}}-1 folds, refit the winner on them, and score it on the outer held-out fold, which the search never saw;

the average of the K_{\text{out}} outer scores estimates the performance of the procedure.

In scikit-learn nesting is a one-liner: the GridSearchCV object is itself an estimator, and it is handed to cross_val_score.

Chapter 5 gave us the rules for evaluating and tuning a model, and used logistic regression and its hyperparameter C as a black box. This chapter opens the box. It starts from the least-squares fit of Chapter 1, shows precisely when it is reliable and when it is not, repairs it with ridge regression, and then builds the classification counterpart: logistic regression, its regularized version, and its extension to more than two classes. Along the way, the tools of the previous chapters (the bias–variance decomposition of Chapter 4, the norm bounds of Chapter 3, the cross-validation of Chapter 5) turn into theorems and measurements about a concrete family of models.

After working through it you will be able to:

state and prove the statistical properties of least squares: it is unbiased, its covariance is \sigma^2(\mathbf{X}^\top\mathbf{X})^{-1}, it is the best linear unbiased estimator (Gauss–Markov), and each parameter costs exactly \sigma^2/n of prediction error and 2\sigma^2/n of training-error optimism;

compute a least-squares solution stably (QR or SVD instead of the normal equations), and diagnose collinearity with the variance inflation factor;

derive ridge regression, express it with the singular value decomposition, define its effective degrees of freedom, compute its bias and variance exactly, and prove that some \lambda > 0 always beats least squares in estimation error;

choose \lambda cheaply with the leave-one-out shortcut, and recognize the situations in which regularization does not help;

derive logistic regression as maximum likelihood for a Bernoulli model, prove that its loss is convex, and implement Newton’s method (IRLS) from scratch;

explain what happens when the classes are linearly separable (the maximum-likelihood solution does not exist), how an \ell_2 penalty repairs it, and how \lambda relates to scikit-learn’s C;

extend the model to many classes with the softmax function, and inspect its weights as images;

obtain nonlinear models with linear tools through feature maps, see why regularization is indispensable when the number of features is large, and follow a complete protocol for linear models.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 5. In particular the normal equations and the geometry of Section 1.6, maximum likelihood and the Gaussian-noise view of least squares (Section 2.6.7), the bias–variance decomposition (Section 4.5), the norm bounds of Section 3.8, the tuning protocols of Section 5.4, and the calibration of probabilities of Section 5.6.4.

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets: Diabetes, Breast Cancer, Digits and, for one demonstration, two classes of Iris, all shipped with scikit-learn. Three experiments need a situation in which the truth is known exactly, and they are flagged where they appear: a simulation with Gaussian designs that checks a formula (Section 6.2.1), a numerical experiment with a matrix of monomials (Section 6.2.3), and a simulated world built on the real Diabetes design in which the true coefficients are known (Section 6.3.3). In every other experiment, the data are real and the numbers that we report are measured, not simulated.

Every model of the book so far has been defined as the minimizer of a loss. Chapters 1 and 6 could find that minimizer by solving linear equations, or, for the logistic loss, with a few Newton steps. In the chapters that follow (support vector machines, boosting, neural networks) the minimizer has to be computed iteratively, with methods that use only the gradient of the loss, because the Hessian is too large to form, or because the loss is a sum over millions of examples. This chapter develops the theory and the practice of these methods on the models that we already understand, where the true minimizer is known and every claim can be checked.

After working through it you will be able to:

derive the gradient of the least-squares, logistic and softmax losses, verify it by finite differences, and prove that the negative gradient is the direction of steepest descent;

define smoothness (L), strong convexity (\mu) and the condition number \kappa = L/\mu, and compute them for the models of Chapter 6;

state and prove the descent lemma, and from it the convergence of gradient descent: exact formulas on quadratics (with the step-size limit 2/L), the O(1/k) rate for convex smooth functions, and the linear rate (1 - \mu/L)^k for strongly convex ones;

explain why feature scaling speeds up the optimization (preconditioning), why Newton’s method is the ideal preconditioner, and choose a step size automatically with backtracking line search;

derive momentum (heavy ball) and Nesterov acceleration, and prove that on quadratics they replace \kappa by \sqrt\kappa;

explain stochastic gradient descent: the unbiased mini-batch gradient, the noise floor of a constant step size, the O(1/k) rate of a decreasing step size, and the cost accounting in epochs;

use SGD in practice: choose the learning rate on a logarithmic grid, and recognize early stopping as an implicit form of ridge regularization and gradient descent on separable data as a path to the maximum-margin classifier;

describe adaptive methods (AdaGrad, RMSprop, Adam) and what they fix and do not fix, understand the role of saddle points in non-convex problems, and choose an optimizer for a given problem.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 6. In particular the normal equations (Section 1.6), the eigendecomposition and the effect of feature scales (Sections 2.3.7 and 2.7.1), the conditioning of least squares (Section 6.2.3), the singular-value view of ridge (Section 6.3.2), and the logistic loss, Newton’s method and separable data (Sections 6.4.2 to 6.4.4).

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets, all shipped with scikit-learn: Diabetes (least squares), Breast Cancer (logistic regression), Digits (softmax regression) and two classes of Iris (a separable problem). Since the theory of this chapter is about optimization, the target of each experiment is the exact minimum of the loss, which we compute once with Newton’s method or with L-BFGS and call f^\ast; the plots then show the suboptimality f(\mathbf{w}_k) - f^\ast, which can be measured exactly. Only two constructions are not data: the small quadratic functions used to draw the geometry of gradient descent (Section 7.3.2), and two classical test functions of non-convex optimization (Section 7.9). Both are flagged where they appear.

Chapters 3 and 6 gave two separate reasons to look for a large margin. Theorem 3.7 bounds the error of a linear classifier by the fraction of training points below a margin \gamma plus a term proportional to \lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert/\gamma, so that a classifier with a large margin and a small norm generalizes well whatever the dimension. Section 6.4.4 found that on separable data the logistic regression has no maximum-likelihood estimate and that the weights diverge, and Section 7.7.3 showed where the direction of the divergence leads. The support vector machine (SVM) turns this around: instead of waiting for an optimizer to find a large margin, it asks for the largest margin directly, as the solution of a convex optimization problem.

After working through the chapter you will be able to:

define the geometric margin of a hyperplane and formulate the hard-margin SVM as the quadratic program “minimize \tfrac12\lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert^2 subject to y_i(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_i + b)\ge 1”;

derive the dual problem and the KKT conditions, and use them to show that \mathbf{w} is a combination of the support vectors only, that the margin is 2/\lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert and that \lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert^2 = \sum_i\alpha_i;

solve a small SVM by hand, through the guess of the support set and the verification of the KKT conditions;

extend the formulation to non-separable data with slack variables, show that it is the minimization of the hinge loss plus a ridge penalty with \lambda = 1/(Cn), and interpret the three kinds of examples (\alpha_i = 0, 0 < \alpha_i < C, \alpha_i = C);

explain what the hinge loss estimates (the Bayes classifier itself, not the probabilities) and compare it with the logistic loss;

prove the convergence rate of the subgradient method with the step 1/(\lambda k), derive the SMO update, and implement both from scratch;

state and prove the leave-one-out bound \widehat{\mathrm{err}}_{\mathrm{LOO}}\le\#\mathrm{SV}/n, evaluate the margin bound of Chapter 3 on a real data set and choose C with the protocols of Chapter 5;

use SVMs in practice with scikit-learn (feature scaling, comparison with logistic regression, probabilities by Platt scaling, multiclass by one-vs-one and one-vs-rest), and see how the dual form leads to kernels (Chapter 9) and to support vector regression.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 7. In particular the margin bound of Section 3.8.4, the cross-validation protocols and McNemar’s test of Chapter 5, regularized logistic regression and the meaning of C (Section 6.5), the dual form of ridge regression (Proposition 6.12), and the implicit bias of gradient descent (Section 7.7.3). Constrained optimization is used through Lagrange multipliers; every step is derived here.

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets, all shipped with scikit-learn: Iris (two petal measurements, for pictures in the plane), Breast Cancer (the main example: the stratified split of Chapters 6 and 7, with 426 training and 143 test patients, malignant = 1), Digits (multiclass) and Diabetes (regression). The only constructions that are not data are the five points of Worked example 8.1, small enough to be solved by hand, and the explicit-feature check of Section 8.8, which is a numerical identity. In this chapter the two classes are labeled y_i \in \{-1, +1\}, as in Section 3.8.4, and the score of an example is f(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x} + b with the intercept b kept apart from \mathbf{w} (unlike Chapter 7, where it was one of the coordinates).

Real data are rarely separable, and even when they are, as the Breast Cancer training set of Section 6.4.4, a hyperplane squeezed between the classes with a tiny margin can be a poor classifier (Exercise 7.10 and its solution showed it: the limit of gradient descent, the hard-margin classifier, has a lower test accuracy than the early-stopped iterates). The remedy is to allow some constraints to be violated, at a price. Introduce a slack variable \xi_i \ge 0 for every example, which measures by how much the constraint of example i is violated, and penalize their sum:\boxed{\ \min_{\mathbf{w}, b, \boldsymbol\xi}\ \ \tfrac12\lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert^2 + C\sum_{i=1}^n\xi_i \quad\text{subject to}\quad y_i(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_i + b)\ \ge\ 1 - \xi_i,\quad \xi_i\ge 0.\ }
\tag{soft-margin primal}

The constant C > 0 sets the price of a violation: a large C approaches the hard-margin problem, a small C tolerates many violations in exchange for a wider margin. An example with \xi_i = 0 is on the right side of its margin hyperplane; with 0 < \xi_i \le 1 it is inside the strip but still correctly classified; with \xi_i > 1 it is misclassified. For fixed (\mathbf{w}, b) the smallest feasible slack is the amount by which the constraint fails, \xi_i = \max\{0,\ 1 - y_i(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_i + b)\}, and substituting it gives an unconstrained problem.

Proposition 8.3 (the soft-margin SVM is regularized hinge-loss minimization)

Define the hinge loss of a margin m = y\,f(\mathbf{x}) as \ell_{\text{hinge}}(m) = \max\{0,\ 1 - m\}. The soft-margin problem is equivalent to\min_{\mathbf{w}, b}\ \ \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\ell_{\text{hinge}}\big(y_i(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{x}_i + b)\big) + \frac\lambda2\lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert^2,
\qquad
\boxed{\ \lambda = \frac{1}{C\,n}\ }

in the sense that both have the same minimizers (the objective of the first is Cn times the objective of the second).

Proof. For fixed (\mathbf{w}, b) the slacks enter the objective of the primal with a positive coefficient and are constrained from below by \xi_i\ge 0 and \xi_i\ge 1 - y_if(\mathbf{x}_i), so the minimum over \boldsymbol\xi is attained at \xi_i = \max\{0, 1 - y_if(\mathbf{x}_i)\} and the objective becomes \tfrac12\lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert^2 + C\sum_i\ell_{\text{hinge}}(m_i). Dividing by Cn gives the second form. \square

This is the same relation as in Section 6.5.1 (\lambda = 1/(Cn)), and it makes the point of the chapter in one line: the SVM is a regularized linear classifier, exactly like the ridge-penalized logistic regression, with the hinge loss in place of the logistic loss. The two losses are compared in Section 8.4. Everything that Chapter 6 said about C holds here: small C is strong regularization, a wide margin and a rigid model; large C is weak regularization, a narrow margin and a flexible model; and C must be chosen with the protocols of Chapter 5.

Section 6.7 improved linear models by adding nonlinear features (all the monomials up to a given degree), and found a limit: the number of features grows quickly with the degree and with the number of inputs, and a fit with many features is a disaster without regularization. Section 6.7.2 and Chapter 8 showed that the computation can be moved to the dual, where the cost depends on n and not on the number of features. The remaining step is to stop building the features at all.

After working through the chapter you will be able to:

define a kernel as an inner product in a feature space, and write the polynomial, Gaussian (RBF), Laplacian and linear kernels, together with the distances and angles that a kernel defines;

state and prove the characterization of kernels (a symmetric function is a kernel if and only if all its Gram matrices are positive semidefinite) and the closure properties (sums, products, exponentials), and use them to show that the Gaussian kernel has an infinite-dimensional feature space, which you can write explicitly in one dimension;

check numerically that a function is, or is not, a kernel, and explain what can go wrong with a function that is not (the dual problem is no longer convex);

define the reproducing kernel Hilbert space of a kernel, state and prove the representer theorem, and use it to turn a penalized problem over functions into a problem in n coefficients;

derive kernel ridge regression, show that it reduces to the ridge regression of Chapter 6 for the linear kernel, prove the leave-one-out shortcut for it, and read the shrinkage of the eigenvalues of the Gram matrix as the shrinkage of the singular values of Section 6.3.2;

use the kernel SVM (the SMO of Section 8.5.2 works on any Gram matrix), tune (C, \gamma) with the protocols of Chapter 5, and describe the three regimes of the bandwidth \gamma: a nearly linear model, a good fit, and memorization;

bound the generalization of a kernel classifier through the norm \lVert f\rVert_{\mathcal{H}} and the radius \sup_x\sqrt{k(x, x)} (Theorem 3.7 in the feature space);

approximate a kernel with random Fourier features (prove that they are unbiased and give a Hoeffding bound) or with the Nyström method, and say when the approximation is worth it.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 8. In particular feature maps and the dual form of ridge regression (Sections 6.7.1 and 6.7.2), the singular-value view of ridge (Section 6.3.2) and its leave-one-out shortcut (Proposition 6.8), the margin and Rademacher bounds of Section 3.8, the dual of the SVM and the function smo (Sections 8.2 to 8.5), and the cross-validation protocols of Chapter 5. Linear algebra of symmetric positive semidefinite matrices (Section 2.3.7) is used throughout.

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets, all shipped with scikit-learn: Iris (the Gram matrices of Section 9.3), Breast Cancer (the stratified split of Chapters 6 to 8, 426 training and 143 test patients, malignant = +1), Digits (the stratified split of 1347 training and 450 test images) and Diabetes (the 331/111 split of Chapters 6 and 7). Only one example is not data: the three points of Worked example 9.1, small enough to be solved by hand. The features of the Breast Cancer data are standardized with the statistics of the training set, as in Chapter 8; for Digits we shall see that the scale of the features matters for kernels more than for linear models.

A kernel encodes what “similar” means for the data at hand, and Proposition 9.2 gives a recipe book for building new ones from known ones, which will be valid by construction. Sums of kernels act on different groups of features or different views of the data (a sum of kernels is a kernel on the concatenated feature spaces); products combine conditions; normalizing k(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{z})/\sqrt{k(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x})k(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z})} gives a kernel with unit images; and the same machinery covers inputs that are not vectors (strings, graphs, trees, images), where the kernel is the way of introducing the knowledge about the problem (Chapter 17 and Chapter 21 will meet some of them, in the guise of convolutions and graph Laplacians).

In scikit-learn any kernel can be used with SVC(kernel="precomputed"): the training matrix \mathbf{K} is passed to fit, and the matrix of the kernel values between the test and the training points to predict. The first thing to check is that nothing changes with a built-in kernel. The second is an illustration of Proposition 9.2 on the Breast Cancer data, whose 30 features are the mean, the standard error and the worst value of ten measurements: we compare the Gaussian kernel on all the features, the sum of three Gaussian kernels, one for each group of ten features, and their product (which by Proposition 9.2(ii) and the factorization of the exponential is exactly the Gaussian kernel on all the features, if the bandwidth is the same).

K_tr_pre = rbf_kernel(Zb, gamma=g_star)
pre_svc = SVC(kernel="precomputed", C=C_star, tol=1e-10).fit(K_tr_pre, yb_tr)
builtin = SVC(kernel="rbf", gamma=g_star, C=C_star, tol=1e-10).fit(Zb, yb_tr)
diff_pre = np.abs(pre_svc.decision_function(rbf_kernel(Zb_te, Zb, gamma=g_star)) - builtin.decision_function(Zb_te)).max()
print(f"precomputed kernel against the built-in Gaussian kernel: largest difference of the decision functions {diff_pre:.1e}")

groups = [slice(0, 10), slice(10, 20), slice(20, 30)]                       # mean, standard error, worst value


def k_single(A, B, g):
    return rbf_kernel(A, B, gamma=g)


def k_sum(A, B, g):
    return sum(rbf_kernel(A[:, s], B[:, s], gamma=g) for s in groups)


def k_product(A, B, g):
    return np.prod([rbf_kernel(A[:, s], B[:, s], gamma=g) for s in groups], axis=0)


rows = []
for name, kern in [("Gaussian on all 30 features", k_single), ("sum of three Gaussians (one per group)", k_sum), ("product of three Gaussians", k_product)]:
    best = None
    for g in [0.003, 0.01, 0.03, 0.1, 0.3]:
        K_ = kern(Zb, Zb, g)
        for C in [0.1, 1, 10, 100]:
            accs = []
            for tr, va in skf10.split(Zb, yb_tr):
                m_ = SVC(kernel="precomputed", C=C, tol=1e-4).fit(K_[np.ix_(tr, tr)], yb_tr[tr])
                accs.append(m_.score(K_[np.ix_(va, tr)], yb_tr[va]))
            if best is None or np.mean(accs) > best[0]:
                best = (np.mean(accs), g, C)
    acc_cv, g, C = best
    K_ = kern(Zb, Zb, g)
    final = SVC(kernel="precomputed", C=C, tol=1e-6).fit(K_, yb_tr)
    rows.append({"kernel": name, "gamma": g, "C": C, "10-fold accuracy": acc_cv, "test accuracy": final.score(kern(Zb_te, Zb, g), yb_te),
                 "smallest eigenvalue of the Gram matrix": np.linalg.eigvalsh(K_).min()})
print(pd.DataFrame(rows).to_string(index=False, float_format=lambda v: f"{v:.4g}"))
g_cmp = 0.03
print(f"product kernel against the Gaussian kernel on all the features (same gamma = {g_cmp}): largest difference of the Gram matrices "
      f"{np.abs(k_product(Zb, Zb, g_cmp) - k_single(Zb, Zb, g_cmp)).max():.1e}")





The precomputed kernel reproduces the built-in one to 7\cdot 10^{-14}. The experiment on the groups confirms the algebra of Proposition 9.2: the product of the three Gaussian kernels is exactly the Gaussian kernel on the 30 features (the Gram matrices differ by 2\cdot 10^{-15}, and the tuned models are identical: 10-fold accuracy 0.9789 and test accuracy 0.979), because e^{-\gamma\lVert\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z}\rVert^2} = \prod_ge^{-\gamma\lVert\mathbf{x}_g - \mathbf{z}_g\rVert^2}. The sum is a different kernel (it adds the three feature spaces instead of multiplying them: a function that is a sum of functions of the groups) and it is valid, with a positive smallest eigenvalue (2.6\cdot 10^{-5}), but it is not better on these data: 10-fold accuracy 0.9765 and test accuracy 0.965 against 0.9789 and 0.979 (2 more errors among 143 patients, within the noise). Prior knowledge is only useful when it is correct, and here the correct statement is that the three groups are strongly redundant (the mean and the worst value of a measurement carry nearly the same information, Section 6.2.4).

Chapter 4 showed that irrelevant features hurt a method based on distances, and Section 6.2.4 that nearly collinear features make the individual coefficients of least squares meaningless. Ridge regression handles both by shrinking, but it never says which features matter. Here we ask the model to say it.

After working through the chapter you will be able to:

explain why a model that uses few features can be preferable (interpretation and cost, and also estimation error), and write the sparse learning problem with the count \lVert\mathbf{w}\rVert_0 of non-zero weights;

carry out best-subset selection exhaustively on a small problem, explain why the training error cannot choose the size of the subset, choose it with C_p, BIC and cross-validation, and prove the covariance form of the optimism that shows why the effective degrees of freedom of a searched subset exceed its size;

derive forward stepwise selection and orthogonal matching pursuit, and prove that the latter recovers the true support under the exact-recovery condition, and under a bound on the coherence of the features;

define the lasso, derive soft-thresholding for an orthonormal design, prove the optimality conditions and the value of \lambda above which the solution is zero, and read the penalty geometrically (the corners of the \ell_1 ball) and as a Laplace prior;

derive and implement coordinate descent and proximal gradient (ISTA and FISTA) for the lasso, prove the O(1/k) rate of the latter, and show on data why the plain subgradient method is a poor choice;

choose \lambda by cross-validation with the one-standard-error rule, interpret the number of non-zero coefficients as the degrees of freedom of the lasso, and prove the slow-rate bound that shows why the number of useless features enters the error only through its logarithm;

explain why the lasso selects arbitrarily among correlated features, define the elastic net, and prove the grouping effect;

fit an \ell_1-penalized logistic regression, see that the representer theorem of Chapter 9 fails for the \ell_1 penalty, and perform feature selection inside the cross-validation loop, measuring the leak of selecting outside it and the invalid p-values that follow a selection.

Prerequisites. Chapters 1 to 9. In particular the leakage and the pipelines of Section 2.7, the \ell_1 and \ell_2 Rademacher complexities of Section 3.8 and Exercise 3.9, the irrelevant features of Section 4.7.2, the one-standard-error rule (Section 5.4.1) and nested cross-validation (Section 5.4.3), collinearity and VIF (Section 6.2.4), the optimism of ridge (Proposition 6.5) and the three views of the penalty (Section 6.3.5), regularized logistic regression (Section 6.5), the descent lemma and Nesterov’s acceleration (Proposition 7.2 and Theorem 7.9), and the subgradient and dual coordinate methods of Section 8.5.

A word about the data in this chapter

The experiments use real data sets shipped with scikit-learn: Diabetes (the 331/111 split of Chapters 6, 7 and 9, features standardized with the training statistics), Breast Cancer (the stratified 426/143 split of Chapters 6, 8 and 9, malignant =1) and Digits (only the digits 3 and 8 in Sections 10.9 and 10.11). Four kinds of experiment are not plain data, and are flagged where they occur: (i) real features with appended noise columns, as in Section 4.7.2; (ii) a Gaussian design for the support-recovery experiment of Section 10.4, where the true support must be known; (iii) a simulated world on the real Diabetes design, with the least-squares fit as the truth, as in Section 6.3.3, to verify a formula for the degrees of freedom; (iv) shuffled labels on real features, a world in which by construction no feature carries any information about the label. The test patients of Diabetes are used once, in Section 10.7.3.

